            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"Action, personal agency, power, and in relation to God, intimate protection, remembrance, and sovereign care.\",\"primary_basis\": [\"Isaiah 49:16\", \"Psalm 119:109\", \"Judges 6:13\"],\"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"Prayer, supplication, and surrender to God, derived from the physical gesture of spreading out the palms.\",\"basis\": [\"1 Kings 8:22\", \"Psalm 141:2\", \"Exodus 9:29\"],\"prominence\": \"common\"},{\"meaning\": \"Moral innocence, uprightness, and purity of deeds, usually expressed as having 'clean hands'.\",\"basis\": [\"Psalm 24:4\", \"Job 22:30\"],\"prominence\": \"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\": true,\"confidence\": \"high\",\"notes\": \"The Hebrew word 'kaph' denotes the hollow of the hand or palm. While it is frequently translated simply as 'hand' and shares the broad biblical symbolism of power, possession, and human action with 'yad' (hand), 'kaph' carries distinct nuances in specific idioms. These include the spreading of palms in earnest prayer and the intimate, protective imagery of God inscribing His people on the palms of His hands.\"}",
